# =============================================================================
# Merqo — environment variables
#
# Copy to .env.local (git-ignored) and fill in real values:
#     cp .env.example .env.local
#
# RULES (established here, enforced by every later milestone):
#   1. Never commit a real value to this file. Placeholders only.
#   2. Anything named NEXT_PUBLIC_* is shipped to the browser. Never put a
#      secret behind that prefix.
#   3. SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY is server-only and bypasses RLS. It must never
#      be imported from a Client Component, and lives only in Vercel's and
#      GitHub's encrypted secret stores.
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Application — Milestone 01
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Canonical public URL of this deployment.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=http://localhost:3000


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Supabase — Milestone 01 (health check), required from Milestone 02 onward
#
# Hosted values: Supabase Dashboard -> Project Settings -> API
# Local values:  run `pnpm db:start`; the CLI prints a local URL and keys.
#                Use the local ones for day-to-day development.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project-ref.supabase.co
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your-anon-key

# SERVER ONLY — bypasses Row Level Security. See rule 3 above.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=your-service-role-key

# Used by the Supabase CLI for `supabase link` / `supabase db push`.
SUPABASE_PROJECT_REF=your-project-ref

# Database password for the hosted project. Needed from Milestone 02 (migrations).
# SUPABASE_DB_PASSWORD=your-database-password


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Transactional email — Resend (introduced by Milestone 11's employee-invite
# flow; Milestone 12 layers NotificationService on top of the same
# lib/email/service.ts — see that module's doc for the layering).
#
# Optional, not required: leave RESEND_API_KEY unset and lib/email/service.ts
# selects a structured-log transport instead, so a fresh clone and every CI
# job work with zero secrets — the invitation link is always also shown
# directly in the employee directory UI regardless of delivery.
#
# Without a verified sending domain, the shared onboarding@resend.dev sender
# only delivers to the Resend account's own login address — see
# lib/email/transports/resend.ts.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RESEND_API_KEY=your-resend-api-key
# RESEND_FROM_EMAIL=onboarding@resend.dev

# Only used by the opt-in live-delivery test
# (tests/integration/notifications-email.test.ts), Milestone 12's "at least
# one true end-to-end test against Resend's actual API in a controlled test
# environment". Set this AND RESEND_API_KEY to actually send one real email
# during `pnpm test:integration`; with either unset the test skips itself,
# so a fresh clone and CI as it stands today both stay green with zero new
# secrets. Must be the Resend account's own login address unless a verified
# sending domain is configured — see lib/email/transports/resend.ts.
# RESEND_TEST_RECIPIENT=you@example.com


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Subscription billing — Paystack (Milestone 13)
#
# SCOPE: software-subscription billing ONLY. Paystack is never used for POS
# customer payments. See docs/milestones/DECISIONS_AND_CONFLICTS.md.
#
# Optional, not required: leave PAYSTACK_SECRET_KEY unset and
# lib/paystack/service.ts's resolvePaystackTransport() returns null — the
# renew screen shows "online payment is not configured" instead of crashing,
# so a fresh clone and every CI job still work with zero secrets.
#
# PAYSTACK_WEBHOOK_SECRET is reserved for symmetry with the naming every
# other provider integration here uses, but Paystack has no separate webhook
# secret in its product — it signs webhooks with the SECRET KEY itself.
# lib/paystack/service.ts's resolvePaystackWebhookSecret() falls back to
# PAYSTACK_SECRET_KEY when this is unset, so setting it is optional even once
# PAYSTACK_SECRET_KEY is configured.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NEXT_PUBLIC_PAYSTACK_PUBLIC_KEY=your-paystack-public-key
# PAYSTACK_SECRET_KEY=your-paystack-secret-key
# PAYSTACK_WEBHOOK_SECRET=your-paystack-webhook-secret

# The same PAYSTACK_SECRET_KEY above also gates the opt-in live-sandbox test
# (tests/integration/subscription-paystack-live.test.ts), mirroring
# RESEND_TEST_RECIPIENT's pattern: set it to a Paystack TEST-MODE secret key
# to exercise a real initialize/verify round trip against Paystack's sandbox;
# leave it unset and the test skips itself, so CI stays green with zero new
# secrets.


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Scheduled jobs — the daily subscription-expiry sweep (Milestone 13)
#
# app/api/cron/subscriptions/route.ts checks
# `Authorization: Bearer $CRON_SECRET` on every request. Vercel injects that
# exact header automatically once CRON_SECRET is set as a project env var —
# no vercel.json change needed beyond the crons entry already committed.
# Unset = the endpoint refuses to run (503), never runs unauthenticated.
# Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CRON_SECRET=generate-a-random-value-do-not-use-this-placeholder
